00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,840
 Okay, so I wanted to talk a little bit about the NCFCA LD resolution because it's a perfect

2
00:00:05,840 --> 00:00:10,440
 example of a poorly worded resolution that kind of inevitably leads to awkward and confusing

3
00:00:10,440 --> 00:00:11,440
 debates.

4
00:00:11,440 --> 00:00:15,720
 But by analyzing the wording of the resolution, we'll cover one, debating from first principles

5
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:20,440
 like what debate actually is beneath the jargon, two, like how to interpret resolutions and

6
00:00:20,440 --> 00:00:24,280
 three, why burdens determine like the wind conditions.

7
00:00:24,280 --> 00:00:29,900
 So the NCFCA resolution is resolved, it is just for a criminal justice system to value

8
00:00:29,900 --> 00:00:33,040
 retribution over rehabilitation.

9
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:38,000
 It is just for a criminal justice system to value retribution over rehabilitation.

10
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:41,740
 And before we get into the issues I have with this resolution, let's talk about like what

11
00:00:41,740 --> 00:00:43,240
 debate is.

12
00:00:43,240 --> 00:00:47,680
 At the end of the day, no matter what the style of debate you compete in, debate comes down

13
00:00:47,680 --> 00:00:54,260
 to, this is the resolution, it is true because of my thesis and my thesis is true because

14
00:00:54,260 --> 00:00:55,260
 of these warrants.

15
00:00:55,260 --> 00:01:01,280
 Again, all debate, all competitive debate comes down to this resolution is true because

16
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:04,640
 of my thesis and my thesis is true because of these warrants.

17
00:01:04,640 --> 00:01:08,440
 And when we forget that this is what debate is, we begin performing the words without

18
00:01:08,440 --> 00:01:13,640
 understanding the purpose, definitions, resolution analysis, burdens, weighing mechanisms, values,

19
00:01:13,640 --> 00:01:16,480
 criterians, what do they all actually mean, right?

20
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:20,280
 We teach that evidence alone does not make the argument and yet sometimes we miss that

21
00:01:20,280 --> 00:01:23,360
 the jargon itself becomes evidence without substance.

22
00:01:23,360 --> 00:01:27,520
 So let's break down what the resolution is like phrased by phrase.

23
00:01:27,520 --> 00:01:31,200
 It's basically three phrases or four phrases, depending on how you want to break it up.

24
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:35,360
 But first, it is just, this is the initial moral claim and really what the resolution

25
00:01:35,360 --> 00:01:41,640
 hinges on is we're asking the question, is it actually just to make this value, this value

26
00:01:41,640 --> 00:01:42,640
 comparison?

27
00:01:42,640 --> 00:01:45,760
 And then it's for a criminal justice system.

28
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:46,760
 This is the actor.

29
00:01:46,760 --> 00:01:51,280
 So this is allows us to have some context for how we should define what justice is based

30
00:01:51,280 --> 00:01:53,820
 on what the purpose of this actor is.

31
00:01:53,820 --> 00:02:00,520
 And then finally, the moral claim of value, which is to value retribution over rehabilitation.

32
00:02:00,520 --> 00:02:05,680
 It is just the moral claim for a criminal justice system, the actor, to value retribution

33
00:02:05,680 --> 00:02:07,680
 over rehabilitation.

34
00:02:07,680 --> 00:02:10,000
 Now, what actually has to be proved here?

35
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:14,200
 Well, that's the first thing we normally do after we read the resolution, right?

36
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:16,200
 We give the definitions.

37
00:02:16,200 --> 00:02:20,160
 It should go without saying that the reason we define terms is because when I use a word

38
00:02:20,160 --> 00:02:24,340
 and you use that same word, we may not actually mean the same thing.

39
00:02:24,340 --> 00:02:26,360
 This is actually one of the biggest problems in society.

40
00:02:26,360 --> 00:02:31,400
 We argue with each other without realizing that the underlying presuppositions that about

41
00:02:31,400 --> 00:02:33,600
 reality are not the same.

42
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:37,400
 And so don't make the same mistake with LD because otherwise you're missing out on like

43
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:39,200
 the greatest lesson you can learn from it.

44
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:41,520
 This is what I mean by debating from first principles.

45
00:02:41,520 --> 00:02:45,640
 A string of definitions, like it has much less use to the debate round if they cannot

46
00:02:45,640 --> 00:02:48,600
 be synthesized into a thesis that affirms the resolution.

47
00:02:48,600 --> 00:02:50,600
 Now, here's an example of that as the affirmative.

48
00:02:50,600 --> 00:02:52,120
 So like the resolution is resolved.

49
00:02:52,120 --> 00:02:57,560
 It is just for a criminal justice system to value retribution over rehabilitation.

50
00:02:57,560 --> 00:03:02,800
 Just is defined as morally right to values defined as to consider more important.

51
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:06,360
 Retribution is defined as the philosophy that crime is a debt toward society and punishment

52
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:08,520
 is a payment of that crime.

53
00:03:08,520 --> 00:03:13,200
 Rehabilitation is defined as the philosophy that treats crime as a disease that needs

54
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:16,400
 to be healed for the betterment of society.

55
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:18,240
 These are like very loose definitions.

56
00:03:18,240 --> 00:03:21,160
 I wouldn't use these but I'm just trying to paint the picture.

57
00:03:21,160 --> 00:03:23,440
 This debate is about what reflects justice better.

58
00:03:23,440 --> 00:03:27,280
 This means that the definition of justice is what the debate is about.

59
00:03:27,280 --> 00:03:32,120
 A lot of people say I don't like definition debate and I generally don't think they understand

60
00:03:32,120 --> 00:03:36,360
 what value debate is or Lincoln Douglas debate is or really debate as a contract as a whole

61
00:03:36,360 --> 00:03:40,160
 because definitions are the foundation of the framework.

62
00:03:40,160 --> 00:03:43,840
 They determine whether or not your thesis is even valid or not or supports the resolution

63
00:03:43,840 --> 00:03:44,840
 or not.

64
00:03:44,840 --> 00:03:47,280
 So what is the resolution asking us to answer?

65
00:03:47,280 --> 00:03:51,320
 If you say that it's asking us to answer the retribution not to be valued about rehabilitation,

66
00:03:51,320 --> 00:03:52,640
 you would be wrong.

67
00:03:52,640 --> 00:03:57,120
 The wording of this particular resolution is asking us what is just for a criminal justice

68
00:03:57,120 --> 00:03:59,200
 system to operate from.

69
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:03,600
 So my thesis based on the definitions that I provided is it is morally right for a criminal

70
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:08,320
 justice system to prioritize punishing a criminal for their wrongdoing than restoring them to

71
00:04:08,320 --> 00:04:10,120
 society.

72
00:04:10,120 --> 00:04:11,760
 What does that look like in practice?

73
00:04:11,760 --> 00:04:16,040
 Well it could mean that there are times even if a criminal could healthily be reintroduced

74
00:04:16,040 --> 00:04:22,000
 back into society sooner that we would make them serve the full penalty and still be justified.

75
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:28,560
 That is just to allow or even force that criminal to face the entire penalty even if we could

76
00:04:28,560 --> 00:04:32,280
 rehabilitate them into society sooner.

77
00:04:32,280 --> 00:04:37,160
 So why do stating your thesis and knowing your thesis matter?

78
00:04:37,160 --> 00:04:39,600
 Because you have to know what your burden is.

79
00:04:39,600 --> 00:04:40,600
 What are burdens?

80
00:04:40,600 --> 00:04:41,760
 They are the wind conditions.

81
00:04:41,760 --> 00:04:44,680
 Your thesis in every argument is like a three leg table.

82
00:04:44,680 --> 00:04:48,840
 It requires premises and warrants, assumptions to support or hold it up.

83
00:04:48,840 --> 00:04:52,760
 If you remove even one of the legs, you no longer have a valid table.

84
00:04:52,760 --> 00:04:55,040
 So here's why that's important.

85
00:04:55,040 --> 00:05:01,320
 The negative has not actually proven the resolution false by saying both are just.

86
00:05:01,320 --> 00:05:06,960
 Both are just can still include that retribution be valued over rehabilitation.

87
00:05:06,960 --> 00:05:11,080
 If I say sometimes we should value retribution over rehabilitation and sometimes we should

88
00:05:11,080 --> 00:05:15,600
 value rehabilitation over retribution, the statement that it is just to do the first

89
00:05:15,600 --> 00:05:17,440
 has not been negated.

90
00:05:17,440 --> 00:05:21,160
 Now the negative can argue that neither are just, and I'm not saying this is the best

91
00:05:21,160 --> 00:05:23,440
 argument but I'm trying to get you to see how the logic works.

92
00:05:23,440 --> 00:05:28,520
 If neither are just, then it cannot be just to value one over the other, right?

93
00:05:28,520 --> 00:05:33,680
 If both are just, if valuing either higher situationally just, then it is entirely possible

94
00:05:33,680 --> 00:05:35,320
 for the resolution to be true.

95
00:05:35,320 --> 00:05:38,840
 So this is why the negative can't just run a balanced negative and say well both are actually

96
00:05:38,840 --> 00:05:44,280
 good without making an extra claim that it is actually bad to ever value one over the

97
00:05:44,280 --> 00:05:45,280
 other.

98
00:05:45,280 --> 00:05:48,720
 Which is just kind of hard to argue because situationally, we make decisions all the time

99
00:05:48,720 --> 00:05:51,200
 that prioritize one thing over another.

100
00:05:51,200 --> 00:05:53,040
 We're trying to make a normative claim.

101
00:05:53,040 --> 00:05:56,800
 And so that's why I think this resolution is worded poorly because it's very difficult

102
00:05:56,800 --> 00:05:58,480
 for the negative.

103
00:05:58,480 --> 00:06:02,520
 They do not have to prove that rehabilitation is better, but they do have to prove that

104
00:06:02,520 --> 00:06:07,880
 choosing retribution over rehabilitation would not be just, right?

105
00:06:07,880 --> 00:06:13,000
 The negative does not have to prove that rehabilitation is better, but they do have to prove that

106
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:17,460
 choosing retribution over rehabilitation would not be just.

107
00:06:17,460 --> 00:06:20,840
 That's a moral or definitional claim about justice.

108
00:06:20,840 --> 00:06:24,600
 And I think in an effort to try to center the debate around two forms of justice, the

109
00:06:24,600 --> 00:06:28,800
 league actually just wrote a resolution that gave negative almost no, at least rhetorically

110
00:06:28,800 --> 00:06:30,400
 reasonable ground to debate from.

111
00:06:30,400 --> 00:06:34,320
 But here's a case that you can kind of think about as like how I would think about the

112
00:06:34,320 --> 00:06:36,400
 negative or just one example of how to think about the negative.

113
00:06:36,400 --> 00:06:39,920
 There's a kind of two point that I would make, I'm not saying make these your contentions,

114
00:06:39,920 --> 00:06:41,480
 but just kind of hear the idea.

115
00:06:41,480 --> 00:06:44,080
 The first point is that retribution is never fair.

116
00:06:44,080 --> 00:06:47,000
 This idea of retribution is never fair.

117
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:51,120
 The idea that we can like proportionally assign a penalty for a crime is simply delusion.

118
00:06:51,120 --> 00:06:54,800
 Like there's no moral code in the sky we can consult to decide how much we should punish

119
00:06:54,800 --> 00:06:55,800
 a criminal for.

120
00:06:55,800 --> 00:06:58,360
 Instead, we should measure justice based on utility.

121
00:06:58,360 --> 00:07:01,520
 Like why preserve this idea of fairness when it can never be achieved in the first place?

122
00:07:01,520 --> 00:07:06,240
 In fact, it actually leads to abuses, which is like the impact, which is like, for example,

123
00:07:06,240 --> 00:07:07,840
 drug sentencing, right?

124
00:07:07,840 --> 00:07:12,240
 Where political ideology and political motive can be the reason why certain sentences are

125
00:07:12,240 --> 00:07:15,120
 high or low or enforced more or less.

126
00:07:15,120 --> 00:07:20,320
 This actually allows for bad actors to do things in the name of being fair, or the name

127
00:07:20,320 --> 00:07:26,320
 of being retributive, but because you can't actually measure what some one is actually,

128
00:07:26,320 --> 00:07:31,160
 what penalty is actually should be given to a criminal, it just means that we're putting

129
00:07:31,160 --> 00:07:38,320
 this subjective judgment into the hands of individuals in our legal system.

130
00:07:38,320 --> 00:07:42,880
 When we could be basing it on something that's a lot more practical and verifiable, which

131
00:07:42,880 --> 00:07:48,400
 is my second point, that rehabilitation provides achievable targets.

132
00:07:48,400 --> 00:07:50,560
 Rehabilitation provides achievable targets.

133
00:07:50,560 --> 00:07:53,320
 Justice should be defined by things we can actually live out in the real world.

134
00:07:53,320 --> 00:07:58,400
 If we define justice by what good we can do for society by eliminating crime and developing

135
00:07:58,400 --> 00:08:02,480
 healthy citizens, we can actually have metrics and goals we can hold our policies to.

136
00:08:02,480 --> 00:08:06,400
 This is a better standard than a pie in the sky standard of whatever is proportional

137
00:08:06,400 --> 00:08:07,640
 to the crime.

138
00:08:07,640 --> 00:08:08,880
 What is proportional to the crime?

139
00:08:08,880 --> 00:08:10,240
 Who gets to decide?

140
00:08:10,240 --> 00:08:14,280
 This is why we need standards that are accountable to reality, like crime rates, like someone's

141
00:08:14,280 --> 00:08:19,040
 likelihood to commit a crime again, like these are actually useful statistics.

142
00:08:19,040 --> 00:08:22,960
 And then when people actually do commit crimes, again, we can then address them properly in

143
00:08:22,960 --> 00:08:25,200
 order to, again, prevent more future crime.

144
00:08:25,200 --> 00:08:29,360
 This is a much better way of thinking about justice, that justice, this idea that justice

145
00:08:29,360 --> 00:08:34,640
 is about proportional sentencing, it sounds good until you realize that there's no such

146
00:08:34,640 --> 00:08:38,840
 thing as proportional sentencing in the real world because no one actually knows what is

147
00:08:38,840 --> 00:08:41,480
 proportional to each crime.

148
00:08:41,480 --> 00:08:44,040
 That's kind of the approach that I would take as the negative, and that's just my first

149
00:08:44,040 --> 00:08:45,040
 thoughts.

150
00:08:45,040 --> 00:08:46,040
 I'm sure that there are other valid cases.

151
00:08:46,040 --> 00:08:50,680
 There's a lot of different approaches you can take, but I generally think that a lot

152
00:08:50,680 --> 00:08:55,640
 of the times the negatives in this season are going to provide cases that don't actually

153
00:08:55,640 --> 00:08:57,200
 negate the resolution.

154
00:08:57,200 --> 00:09:01,440
 And on the flip side, you're going to have affirmative teams that just define the resolution

155
00:09:01,440 --> 00:09:02,800
 to be true by default as a truism.

156
00:09:02,800 --> 00:09:09,240
 In fact, this is the round that I judged, the only LD round I've judged this year for

157
00:09:09,240 --> 00:09:14,320
 NCFCA, the affirmative basically defined justice as giving people what they deserve, and then

158
00:09:14,320 --> 00:09:19,320
 retribution is defined as giving people what they deserve, or what is proportionate to

159
00:09:19,320 --> 00:09:20,320
 the crime.

160
00:09:20,320 --> 00:09:25,640
 But that means the same thing, so you can't say it's just for retribution to be valued

161
00:09:25,640 --> 00:09:29,560
 over rehabilitation because justice is retribution.

162
00:09:29,560 --> 00:09:33,800
 You didn't actually prove anything anymore, you're just defining yourself into a win because

163
00:09:33,800 --> 00:09:38,720
 you didn't realize that the debate itself is about how we should define justice.

164
00:09:38,720 --> 00:09:43,360
 So this is why it has to be a definition debate, the resolution was framed as a definition

165
00:09:43,360 --> 00:09:46,640
 debate about what is justice.

166
00:09:46,640 --> 00:09:51,200
 You can't just kind of say that justice is the way that we think of justice in a retributive

167
00:09:51,200 --> 00:09:56,200
 system, therefore retribution is more valuable because that's assuming the presuppositions

168
00:09:56,200 --> 00:09:57,200
 of retribution.

169
00:09:57,200 --> 00:10:01,280
 And so this is again why you have to think in terms of a table with three legs, like what

170
00:10:01,280 --> 00:10:06,240
 actually requires, what is actually holding the argument up, what are the burdens.

171
00:10:06,240 --> 00:10:11,160
 And on the flip side of that, that also means that if I know that my thesis is this three

172
00:10:11,160 --> 00:10:15,800
 leg table, and these are the arguments that hold it up, that also means that I know what

173
00:10:15,800 --> 00:10:20,240
 my opponent has to prove in order to make it not a table anymore, right?

174
00:10:20,240 --> 00:10:24,720
 I know what arguments they have to prove to disprove the resolution because it's the

175
00:10:24,720 --> 00:10:29,960
 same foundation or the same warrants or the same arguments that are holding up my thesis.

176
00:10:29,960 --> 00:10:33,720
 So that's why understanding what your thesis is is so important is because it doesn't help

177
00:10:33,720 --> 00:10:35,640
 you to understand your own arguments.

178
00:10:35,640 --> 00:10:39,160
 It helps you understand what your opponent's arguments are too.

179
00:10:39,160 --> 00:10:42,440
 Like if I am saying, it's kind of like an alibi, right?

180
00:10:42,440 --> 00:10:47,720
 Like if I'm saying, if I'm being accused of a crime and it's like, well, this crime

181
00:10:47,720 --> 00:10:53,640
 happened at the mall at 10 p.m., if I'm able to prove that I was actually somewhere else

182
00:10:53,640 --> 00:10:57,600
 at 10 p.m., then I am not the one that committed the crime, right?

183
00:10:57,600 --> 00:11:02,360
 That is the underlying leg of the argument that they're accusing me of committing this

184
00:11:02,360 --> 00:11:03,360
 crime.

185
00:11:03,360 --> 00:11:07,920
 And if you attack the presupposition or the warrant, which is, well, if you were there

186
00:11:07,920 --> 00:11:12,640
 then you have committed the crime, then you've defeated the actual argument.

187
00:11:12,640 --> 00:11:15,840
 This is how we need to understand our thesis and burdens when it comes to the resolution.

188
00:11:15,840 --> 00:11:19,960
 I'm going to wrap this up because I feel like I'm now just kind of rambling, but I hope

189
00:11:19,960 --> 00:11:23,720
 this helped you understand kind of how to interpret or think about debate a little bit

190
00:11:23,720 --> 00:11:28,160
 differently and also how this resolution, how to think about what these words mean and

191
00:11:28,160 --> 00:11:33,960
 how they build an argument and what actually has to be proven true or false to affirm or

192
00:11:33,960 --> 00:11:35,320
 negate the resolution.

